## Non-negotiables

- Pass `model: "opus"` on every worker-agent spawn. Never accept a smaller model silently.
- Never edit, delete, overwrite, stage, or restore any `.env` file (`.env`, `.env.*`, any subfolder).
- Stage files by name only. Never run `git add -A`, `git add .`, or `git add --all`.
- In plan mode (built-in `/plan`), make no edits and call no non-readonly tools.

## How to work

### Plan-first workflow

- Discuss the approach with the user and produce a concrete plan before implementation.
- Wait for explicit approval before changing code.
- Break the approved plan into ordered subtasks. Work them sequentially.
- Skip the plan-first workflow only for: read-only questions, code explanations, single-line typo fixes, and follow-ups on already-approved plans.

### Delegate to worker agents

- Delegate code exploration and implementation to worker agents. Keep your own context focused on planning, review, and integration.
- Custom agents shipped with this rules package auto-route by trigger phrases in their descriptions:
  - `infra-explorer` — read-only investigations, codebase searches >3 queries.
<!-- {{IF HAS_ANSIBLE}} -->
  - `ansible-implementor` — Ansible role and playbook edits.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF HAS_COMPOSE}} -->
  - `compose-implementor` — Docker Compose stack edits.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF HAS_BASH}} -->
  - `script-implementor` — bash script edits under `${CODE_SUBDIR}/scripts/`.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
  - `lint-runner` — runs `bash ${CODE_SUBDIR}/scripts/lint.sh` and classifies failures.
- For tasks outside those roles, fall back to built-ins: `Explore` for read-only searches, `Plan` for architecture/design, `general-purpose` for implementation.
- Always pass `model: "opus"` on the spawn (custom agents above already have this set).
- Run dependent subtasks sequentially. Run independent subtasks in parallel by issuing multiple Agent calls in one message.

### Review every worker output

- Read the diff.
- Confirm the worker stayed inside the scope you assigned.
- Run the relevant verification command for the change.
- Reject and re-delegate if you spot unrelated edits, scope creep, or skipped verification.

## Conventions

These patterns already hold across the codebase — keep matching them.

<!-- {{IF HAS_BASH}} -->
- Bash scripts use `set -euo pipefail`, snake_case function names with a module prefix (e.g., `registry_init`), and a `# === banner ===` header. See ${CITATION_BASH}.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF HAS_ANSIBLE}} -->
- Ansible tasks use FQCN (fully qualified collection name) — write `ansible.builtin.copy`, not bare `copy`. See ${CITATION_ANSIBLE}.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF HAS_COMPOSE}} -->
- Compose files follow the v2 spec — omit the `version:` field. See ${CITATION_COMPOSE}.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF HAS_TERRAFORM}} -->
- Terraform code is split into provider modules and per-environment roots; run `terraform fmt` before commit. See ${CITATION_TERRAFORM}.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF NO_STACKS}} -->
- No recognised stacks detected; add conventions manually once the project picks up bash/ansible/compose/terraform code.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->

## Verification

Run the right check for the surface you touched.

<!-- {{IF HAS_BASH}} -->
- Shell: `bash -n <file>`, then `shellcheck <file>` if it is installed.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF HAS_ANSIBLE}} -->
- Ansible: `ansible-playbook --syntax-check <playbook>`, then `--check` mode.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF HAS_TERRAFORM}} -->
- Terraform: `terraform fmt -check && terraform validate`.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF HAS_COMPOSE}} -->
- Compose: `docker compose -f <file> config`.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF HAS_BASH}} -->
- Whole suite: `bash ${CODE_SUBDIR}/scripts/lint.sh`. Prefer this over running checks one by one.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
<!-- {{IF NO_STACKS}} -->
- No recognised stacks detected; no verification commands apply yet. Add a tool once a stack is in use.
<!-- {{ENDIF}} -->
- If a check cannot run in the current environment, say so explicitly and describe the residual risk.

